        "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function",
        "problem": "If $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for some $x$, then $cos⁡(x+16\\pi )=$",
        "worked_solution": "The period of $y=cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡x=cos⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=8$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡x=cos⁡(x+16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ then $cos⁡(x+16\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$",
